mber of the family and so  when i found out what we all found out  and i believe that the women were  sexually assaulted and raped that  how do you hold on to this and as black  folks it's a conversation we've been  having amongst ourselves a little bit  but i feel like this was the time to  sort of bring it all together at the  time he had been putting sent to prison  what we thought was going to be for but  as you point out in your documentary you  said you are used to difficult  conversations yeah on my show united  shades of america on cnn you know  famously my first episode was with the  ku klux klan so this is like  somehow it's become part of my brand to  have difficult comments what do you want  us to get out of it because listen you  you you really went all the way there in  terms of the people you interviewed and  the things that they said i thought were  stomach turning  some of the things i hadn't heard before  i mean there's a couple things one i  think a lot of cosby's career involves  the history of black america in the 20th  century roland martin makes that point  and i think we don't want to throw that  away because there are important things  like cosby integrating the blacks the  stunt industry for black people yes and  the other part of it is this is bigger  than cosby like powerful men in media  sexually assaulting harassing raping  women is a story that is not  one man one network we all know it  happens all throughout the industry  everywhere where we sit so the idea  being that we have to  figure out how to create a safer  industry in four parts you spoke to a  lot of people um but you didn't speak or  reach out to cosby's camp and they  commented and said that they're having a  bigger statement tomorrow  but basically called this baseless uh  and had problems with the fact that they  didn't um  have any input i guess in this film i  mean uh bill cosby and his camp has been  very clear about where they stand on  this and this to me was about 